dsh-superagent — Windows adaptation
====================================

This repository is an adaptation built on the official DeepSeek project:

    dsh-superagent-prototype
    https://github.com/deepseek-ai/dsh-superagent-prototype
    Copyright (c) 2026 DeepSeek Harness Team
    Licensed under the MIT License (see LICENSE)

The upstream project is a research prototype, published with the disclaimer quoted in README.md:
it is not an official DeepSeek product, and the design discussed in the article
"Exploring Super-agent Design: Towards a More Autonomous Agent System" is partly future work.

What this repository adds on top of upstream (all MIT, all opt-in or documented):

  * a native Windows desktop application (Electron shell, portable layout, bundled git/gh/pwsh,
    installer and shortcuts), under windows-app/ — removed from this repository on 2026-11-07;
    the credit below is retained as provenance for the removed material
  * multi-instance support: one context boundary per instance, separate state directories and
    per-instance credentials (the desktop management window belonged to the removed shell)
  * the external control loop ("Watchdog") deepened beyond upstream: cross-tick diffing, stall
    detection, silence when nothing changed, and a standing instruction channel
  * Router sharding (article §2.1), off by default: stable Thread placement, shard Sessions created
    on first delivery, visible in health
  * documentation for both the Windows port and the conformance review (docs/011–docs/015、017)

Upstream files keep their copyright and MIT license. Where this adaptation changes upstream behaviour it
is listed in docs/WINDOWS-DELTA.md; when upstream implements the same mechanism, this adaptation deletes its
own version and returns to the upstream implementation.

Provenance of the Windows work
------------------------------

This repository is maintained independently and is not a downstream of anyone else's fork. It builds on
three separate sources, and the split is honest about which parts came from where:

  * the official prototype above — the domain model, engine, sources, prompts and panel;
  * Check-mj's Windows port (https://github.com/Check-mj/dsh-superagent-windows) — portions of the Windows
    layer were first written there: `windows-app/` (the Electron shell, installer, bundled tools, instance
    manager; removed from this repository on 2026-11-07) and docs/011–docs/015、017. That author's work is MIT, like this repository; it is credited here
    and was the origin of that material, but this repository keeps an independent history and its own
    direction, so it is not a fork of that project and no commit history was carried over into the public baseline;
  * the external-agent-orchestration discipline — the operator-side rules injected into the team prompt
    sections (secret redaction, "empty output means unproven", verifiable completion criteria, directory
    boundaries, commit identity), plus Mode A (the local inbox Source) and the outer watchdog/guard layer,
    both designed here.

